IMPORT prelude

-- Acts Interpretation Act 1901 (Australia): PART 8, the section pass 1 left unencoded (pass 2).
-- LAYER 1: the law, objectively. Pass 1 mentioned s 37 in the comments of au-interpretation-time.l4 but gave it no rule, as "no input
-- from which an answer differs". That is not a ground for leaving a provision out, and s 37 does fix an answer: which time a
-- reference to time in an Act means.

§ `Part 8: Distance, time and age (section 37)`

§§ `Section 37: expressions of time`

-- The legal time of a place is an INPUT (a name such as "Australian Eastern Standard Time"); the Act does not list the legal times, and
-- a time zone is not modelled elsewhere in this encoding. What the Act "specifically" states is a MAYBE: NOTHING when it states no time.
-- src:823 | 37 Expressions of time
-- src:824 | Where in an Act any reference to time occurs, such time shall, unless it is otherwise specifically stated, be deemed in each State or part of the Commonwealth to mean the legal time in that State or part of the Commonwealth.
@ref Acts Interpretation Act 1901 s 37 (src:823-824)
GIVEN `the time the Act specifically states` IS A MAYBE STRING
      `the legal time in the State or part of the Commonwealth concerned` IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`the time a reference to time in an Act means` `the time the Act specifically states` `the legal time in the State or part of the Commonwealth concerned` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the time the Act specifically states`
    WHEN JUST stated THEN stated
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the legal time in the State or part of the Commonwealth concerned`
